What should happen?

Because we've deliberately configured the links differently:

             5 ms          5 ms
       ┌──── s2 ───────────┐
       │                   │
h1 ── s1                   s4 ── h2
       │                   │
       └──── s3 ───────────┘
            10 ms         10 ms

Path:

s1 → s2 → s4

has approximately:

$$ 5+5=10ms $$

one-way configured link delay.

Path:

s1 → s3 → s4

has approximately:

$$ 10+10=20ms $$

one-way configured link delay.

Ping measures round-trip time (RTT), not one-way delay, so don't expect the displayed average to be exactly 10 ms.

You may see something roughly like:

================================
MedRoute SDN QoS Measurements
================================
Packet Loss      : 0.0 %
Minimum Latency  : 20.xxx ms
Average Latency  : 21.xxx ms
Maximum Latency  : 23.xxx ms
Individual RTTs  : [...]
================================

Exact values will differ.

Why did we deliberately add delay?

Because previously our Mininet links were essentially:

delay ≈ 0

That makes demonstrating intelligent routing based on latency difficult.

Now we have two genuinely different network conditions:

LOW-DELAY PATH

s1 → s2 → s4
5ms + 5ms

versus:

HIGH-DELAY PATH

s1 → s3 → s4
10ms + 10ms

Later, our SDN controller should understand:

These paths have the same number of hops, but they do not have the same network cost.

That's exactly why we're moving beyond ordinary shortest-path routing.

Important distinction for your review

If your teacher asks:

“Are delay and latency the same?”

You can say:

Delay refers to the time required for a packet to travel through the network. In our experiment, Mininet's TCLink introduces controlled link delays. Latency/RTT is measured using ICMP ping from the source to the destination and represents the round-trip communication time.

And if they ask:

“Is this AI?”

No.

The pipeline is:

Mininet TCLink
      ↓
Real packet transmission
      ↓
ICMP measurement
      ↓
Latency
      ↓
SDN cost calculation
      ↓
Ryu routing decision
      ↓
OpenFlow rules

Entirely SDN/network measurement based.

